> I would like to improve the speed of the displays. What are the most high impact ways I can do that?
**assistant**:
> The biggest wins are avoiding repeated I/O/geometry serialization, and fixing global mutable state.
> 
> 1. **Load and preprocess each dataset and shapefile once**, outside `server()`, or cache it with `memoise::memoise()`. Currently every map update runs `st_read()`; channel changes also re-read CSVs. Read, transform units, add `PID`, and precompute limits at startup.
> 
>    ```r
>    risk_data <- lapply(names(datasets), \(ch) read_and_transform_risk(ch))
>    shapes <- lapply(unique(regions), \(f) sf::st_read(f, quiet = TRUE))
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Do not reload the shapefile for year / quantile / scenario changes.** Your `observe()` calls `load.channel.shapefile(lst$channel)` on *every* relevant input change. Cache it by channel.
> 
> 3. **Avoid re-sending polygon geometries on every map update.** `addPolygons(data = spdf, ...)` serializes all UK geometry to the browser each time year/probability changes. This is likely your dominant display cost.
>    - Add polygons only when the channel changes.
>    - For year/quantile/scenario updates, update polygon styles/tooltips client-side via JavaScript/custom messages, or at least simplify geometries with `st_simplify()`.
>    - Ensure geographic data are WGS84 (`st_transform(4326)`) and remove unused columns before passing to Leaflet.
> 
> 4. **Simplify polygon geometry.** GADM level-3 boundaries can be unnecessarily detailed for a 500px map.
> 
>    ```r
>    spdf <- spdf |>
>      st_transform(4326) |>
>      st_simplify(dTolerance = 0.002) |>
>      dplyr::select(PID, NAME_3, geometry)
>    ```
> 
>    Choose tolerance visually; preserve topology if needed.
> 
> 5. **Precompute map-ready and time-series-ready data.** Each update repeatedly filters, joins, formats confidence intervals, and aggregates. Precompute:
>    - values indexed by `channel/scenario/measure/year`;
>    - national time series by `channel/scenario/measure`;
>    - regional time series by `channel/scenario/measure/region`.
> 
>    `split()` or a keyed `data.table` can make lookup effectively instantaneous.
> 
> 6. **Replace `sapply(... format_estimate_with_ci(...))` with vectorized formatting** and generate tooltips only when necessary. HTML labels for every polygon are expensive.
> 
> 7. **Debounce map updates**, especially sliders:
> 
>    ```r
>    map_inputs <- reactive({
>      list(year = input$year[1], quant = input$quant, ...)
>    }) |> debounce(150)
>    ```
> 
> 8. **Eliminate global `<<-` state.** `df`, `limits`, `mypalette`, `previous`, and `timeseries.id` can leak across concurrent users and cause incorrect maps. Put session-specific state in `reactiveValues()`, while keeping immutable cached source data globally.
> 
> Also profile before/after with `profvis::profvis()` and Shiny’s reactlog. I would prioritize: **cached/simplified shapes → no geometry resend for styling updates → precomputed data summaries**.
